Subject: [PATCH] drm/amdgpu: on a runtime resume, report a hotplug only if a connector changed

This is an InterGenOS patch. It is not an upstream commit and it is not
carried by linux-6.18.10.

amdgpu_device_resume() sends a hotplug event unconditionally on the DC
path. A runtime resume is not a hotplug: a graphics card that drives no
display is resumed by whatever opens its render node, and the event that
followed made every compositor on the machine re-probe its outputs and
rebuild its monitor configuration although no connector had changed.
Measured on a two-card workstation running 6.18.10: the card that serves
compute work runtime-suspends five seconds after its last client closes
it, and each subsequent open produced two change uevents carrying
HOTPLUG=1 for a card with nothing connected to it. The compositor driving
the other card then reopened its own device, and a transient failure in
that reopen cost the user the whole desktop layout.

Upstream posted a fix for this class in December 2021 - Alex Deucher,
"drm/amdgpu: disable hotplug events on runtime pm resume", which skipped
both calls when adev->in_runpm was set. It was never merged. What was
merged instead is commit 4020c2280233 ("drm/amdgpu: don't runtime suspend
if there are displays attached (v3)"), which refuses to runtime suspend a
card that has a display connected, and says why in its own comment, still
present in 6.18.10 in amdgpu_runtime_idle_check_display():

	/* XXX: Return busy if any displays are connected to avoid
	 * possible display wakeups after runtime resume due to
	 * hotplug events in case any displays were connected while
	 * the GPU was in suspend.  Remove this once that is fixed.
	 */

So the display-attached case is covered by not suspending at all, and the
underlying behaviour was left in place with a note asking for it to be
fixed. A card with no display attached is exactly the card that guard
allows to suspend, and it is the case that still emits the spurious event.

Suppressing the event outright would lose a real hotplug: a display
connected while the card was suspended raises no interrupt, and the event
on resume is what tells user space to look. So ask the connectors instead.
On a runtime resume, probe each connector and send the event only when a
connector's epoch counter moved, which is the same test
drm_helper_hpd_irq_event() applies. A display connected during the suspend
still produces an event, because the probe sees it.

Only the DC path is changed. The non-DC path already calls
drm_helper_hpd_irq_event(), which probes before it reports, so it never
had this defect. drm_helper_hpd_irq_event() cannot be used for the DC path:
it returns early unless dev->mode_config.poll_enabled is set, and the DC
display block never calls drm_kms_helper_poll_init(), so on DC hardware it
would report nothing at all - a silent suppression rather than a decision.

The probe runs inside the existing power.disable_depth bracket, which the
surrounding comment says exists so that connector probing from a runtime PM
callback does not deadlock on runtime PM references.

Signed-off-by: InterGenJLU <InterGenJLU@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_device.c
@@ -5283,6 +5283,47 @@
 }
 
 /**
+ * amdgpu_device_hotplug_event_if_changed - report a hotplug only when one happened
+ *
+ * @dev: drm dev pointer
+ *
+ * A runtime resume is not a hotplug. The device is woken by whatever opens it,
+ * which on a card that drives no display is any render client, and the
+ * unconditional hotplug event sent afterwards makes user space re-probe and
+ * rebuild the whole monitor configuration although no connector changed. The
+ * driver already carries this reading in amdgpu_runtime_idle_check_display(),
+ * which refuses to runtime suspend a GPU with a display attached "to avoid
+ * possible display wakeups after runtime resume due to hotplug events" and asks
+ * for the underlying behaviour to be fixed.
+ *
+ * Ask the connectors instead and report only a real change. A display connected
+ * while the device was suspended still produces an event, because the probe
+ * sees it. Only the DC path needs this; the non-DC path already goes through
+ * drm_helper_hpd_irq_event(), which probes before it reports.
+ */
+static void amdgpu_device_hotplug_event_if_changed(struct drm_device *dev)
+{
+	struct drm_connector_list_iter iter;
+	struct drm_connector *connector;
+	bool changed = false;
+
+	mutex_lock(&dev->mode_config.mutex);
+	drm_connector_list_iter_begin(dev, &iter);
+	drm_for_each_connector_iter(connector, &iter) {
+		u64 old_epoch_counter = connector->epoch_counter;
+
+		connector->status = drm_helper_probe_detect(connector, NULL, false);
+		if (connector->epoch_counter != old_epoch_counter)
+			changed = true;
+	}
+	drm_connector_list_iter_end(&iter);
+	mutex_unlock(&dev->mode_config.mutex);
+
+	if (changed)
+		drm_kms_helper_hotplug_event(dev);
+}
+
+/**
  * amdgpu_device_resume - initiate device resume
  *
  * @dev: drm dev pointer
@@ -5378,6 +5419,8 @@
 #endif
 		if (!adev->dc_enabled)
 			drm_helper_hpd_irq_event(dev);
+		else if (adev->in_runpm)
+			amdgpu_device_hotplug_event_if_changed(dev);
 		else
 			drm_kms_helper_hotplug_event(dev);
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM
